# Command Line Tool

<Aside type="caution" title="A concept">

This tool is a concept, and it is optional. You can keep using SSH, the scheduler and your own
editor directly. [RFC 0002](/rfc/0002/) explains why P1 offers it. Commands may change, and parts
are not yet tried on the clusters. Send feedback to #ask-compute or the
[compute needs survey](/survey/).

</Aside>

`aicentre` runs on your laptop and works with P1 compute in three modes:

| Mode | Commands | Where it runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Interactive, CPU | `aicentre shell`, `marimo`, `code` | A login node, at once |
| Interactive, GPU | the same, with `--gpu` | A GPU node, as an interactive job |
| Your jobs | `aicentre list jobs` | Every provider you use, in one table |
| Free GPUs | `aicentre list gpus` | How busy each GPU and queue is |
| Session sizes | `aicentre list sizes` | What `--size` asks for in a `--gpu` session |

`aicentre check` checks your setup. Commands cover every provider you are set up for, and
`--provider gefion` (or `-p gefion`) picks one.

A provider that asks for more than your key, as DTU asks for a password, asks once on your
terminal. The login then stays open for 30 minutes.

## Install

```bash
curl -fsSL https://hpc.aicentre.dk/install | sh
```

It installs the [release](https://github.com/Pioneer-Centre-for-AI/p1-compute-documentation/releases)
for your machine in your home directory, with no admin rights. It runs on Linux and macOS, and in
WSL on Windows. To read the script first, open [/install.txt](/install.txt) in your browser. It is
the same file. Set `AICENTRE_VERSION` for a particular version:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://hpc.aicentre.dk/install | AICENTRE_VERSION=0.1.0 sh
```

With Nix, see [With Nix](#with-nix).

### With your agent

Paste this into Claude Code, Codex or another agent tool. It reads the script first, installs,
runs the check, and asks before it adds `aicentre` as an MCP server.

<!-- md:replace agent-prompt -->
<AgentPrompt />
<!-- md:end -->

## Check your setup

```bash
aicentre check              # the providers you are set up for
aicentre check --all        # every provider
aicentre check --connect    # also log in
```

It checks OpenSSH, `~/.ssh` and `~/.ssh/config`, then each provider:

| Provider | What it checks |
| --- | --- |
| [P1 DTU HPC](/providers/dtu/) | A `Host dtu` block for `login9.hpc.dtu.dk`, your username, an SSH key ssh will use, the host key, and whether the login node answers from where you are |
| [P1 Gefion HPC](/providers/gefion/) | A Host block for the login node, whether your DCAI sign-in login is still valid, and whether the sign-in service answers |
| [P1 NGC HPC](/providers/ngc/) | Nothing to set up here: you work on it through its remote desktop |

A provider is set up when `~/.ssh/config` has its Host block, or for Gefion when you have logged
in to DCAI. `--all` also checks the rest and prints any missing Host block to paste in.

It also tries to reach each login node. If one does not answer, the check warns and says what to look at. `--connect` also logs in, which tests your key and your sign-in. It never prompts, so a login that would ask
fails with a reason. The exit code is 0 when nothing failed and 1 otherwise, and `--json` prints
results for scripts.

When any command does something you did not expect, add `--verbose`. It prints every `ssh` and
`rsync` command aicentre runs, with its exit code and how long it took. Include that output when
you report a problem.

### Settings

Settings are optional. Put names that differ from the defaults in
`~/.config/aicentre/config.toml`. A provider listed there is always checked.

```ini file=~/.config/aicentre/config.toml
[dtu]
user = "s123456"

[gefion]
user = "jdoe"
alias = "gefion"       # the Host name in ~/.ssh/config

# [desktop]
# open = "zed ssh://{host}{urlpath}"   # the editor aicentre code opens

# [session]
# ask = false          # do not ask where to run: the login node unless --gpu says
```

The queue is P1's and needs no setting: `--gpu` sessions go to `p1i` on DTU.

## Your jobs

```bash
aicentre list jobs
```

It shows your running, waiting and suspended jobs across P1 in one table, batch and interactive.
NGC is air-gapped, so its jobs show only in `qstat` on its remote desktop.

```text
Your jobs on P1 compute  3 jobs
  PROVIDER  JOB    STATE    QUEUE  NAME   NODE       ELAPSED  LEFT
  DTU       123    running  p1     train  n-62-30-1  0:05:00  0:55:00
  Gefion    98765  running  p1     eval   dgx-01     2:03:04  21:56:56
  DTU       124    pending  p1     sweep  -          -        -        Not enough GPUs available
```

## Free GPUs

```bash
aicentre list gpus
```

It shows each GPU on DTU with the memory in use and the number of jobs on it, then one line per
queue. On `p1i`, the queue for sessions, the GPUs are shared: a new session starts at once and
shares GPU memory with the others, so look at the memory in use. On `p1`, the batch queue, a GPU
belongs to one job, and the line says how many are free and how many jobs wait. For Gefion it
counts idle nodes per partition.

```text
GPUs on P1 compute  asked DTU

DTU p1i  sessions · 8 running, 0 waiting
  NODE        GPU  MODEL     MEMORY USED  JOBS
  n-62-12-83    0  H100PCIe      35.3 GB     8
  n-62-12-83    1  H100PCIe      48.6 GB     8
  The GPUs are shared: a new session starts at once and shares their memory.

DTU p1  batch jobs · 0 of 14 GPUs free, 92 running, 36 waiting
  NODE        GPU  MODEL     MEMORY USED  JOBS
  n-62-11-44    0  H100PCIe        74 GB     1
  n-62-11-44    1  H100PCIe       3.7 GB     1
```

## Interactive sessions

```bash
aicentre shell                            # a shell on the login node
aicentre marimo project/notebook.py       # marimo in your browser
aicentre jupyter project/notebook.ipynb   # JupyterLab in your browser
aicentre code project                     # VS Code on your desktop, or in your browser
aicentre code project --gpu               # the same, on a GPU node
aicentre marimo train.py --gpu --size large   # with both GPUs and more memory
```

With neither `--gpu` nor `--login`, a session asks in the terminal where to run: the login node
or a GPU session of one of the sizes below. Move with the arrow keys and press Enter, which alone
takes the login node. It asks only when you use DTU, and never in a script, where it takes the
login node.

With `--login`, a session starts at once on your first provider's login node. The login node is
shared, so keep to editing, installs and light runs. A session there runs at low priority and
ends after 12 hours; start it again to go on.

With `--gpu`, it runs as an interactive job on `p1i`, P1 DTU HPC's interactive queue. That queue
is one node with two GPUs that everyone's sessions share, so a session takes at most 4 CPUs,
225 GB and 4 hours, and a second one from your laptop needs `--another`. `--gpu` is for DTU only,
since Gefion runs GPU work as batch jobs and NGC uses its remote desktop.

`--size` picks how much the job asks for. `aicentre list sizes` prints the same table. With
`--gpu` and no `--size`, the question lists only the sizes, and Enter alone takes `small`.

| Size | GPUs | Memory | Time |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `small` (default) | 1 | 32 GB | 2 hours |
| `medium` | 1 | 96 GB | 4 hours |
| `large` | 2 | 200 GB | 4 hours |

A job cannot be given more time once it runs. The terminal rings and tells you when 10 and when
2 minutes are left, so you can save. The job also ends 10 minutes after you close the browser
tab or the editor, which frees the GPU for the next person.

### Python and packages

A session picks its Python from the folder it opens:

- A folder with a `pyproject.toml` runs in that project's environment, through uv.
- A folder with a `.venv` uses that environment.
- Any other folder uses one default environment that all your sessions share, with Python 3.13.

So in a shell, `pip install torch` puts torch where your notebooks in marimo, JupyterLab and
VS Code find it. The shell says which environment it uses when it starts.

`aicentre code` opens notebooks too, Jupyter's and marimo's. It installs the Python, Jupyter and
marimo extensions before the editor opens the first time, and the editor finds the environment
above as a kernel.

VS Code asks whether you trust a folder before it runs code from it. In a folder that only you
can write to, such as your home directory, aicentre turns that question off. In a folder that
others can write to, such as a shared project directory, the question stays: settings that
someone else puts there could otherwise run a program as you.

### What a session installs

The first time, a session needs software on the cluster that is not aicentre's own: uv, Python,
marimo or JupyterLab, VS Code. It lists each one with where it comes from and asks before it
installs anything. Answer yes, or pass `--yes` to agree ahead of time, for a script. Everything
goes into your project directory, under `.aicentre`.

With no path, a session opens your home directory on the cluster, and the ready message names
the folder. Other paths are on the cluster, relative to your project directory
(`/dtu/p1/<username>` on DTU, your home on Gefion) unless absolute or under `~`. Quote a path
under `~`, as in `'~/work/nb.py'`, or your own shell turns it into a path on your machine.

When a relative path is a file or folder on your machine and not on the cluster, the session
asks whether to copy it there first. It never copies over something that is on the cluster
already, and never without asking. Your edits stay on the cluster; `aicentre pull` brings them
back.

| Option | Default | What |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--gpu` | | Run on a GPU node |
| `--login` | | Run on the login node, without the question |
| `--size medium` | `small` | With `--gpu`: `small`, `medium` or `large`, as in the table above |
| `--time 2h` | the size's | With `--gpu`: how long the job may run, as `90`, `2h` or `1:30`; at most 4 hours |
| `--cpus 4` | 4 | With `--gpu`: CPU cores; 4 is the most `p1i` gives |
| `--mem 8GB` | the size's | With `--gpu`: memory per core; at most 225 GB in all |
| `--another` | | With `--gpu`: start although a session of yours already holds a GPU |
| `--yes` | | Install what the session needs on the cluster without asking |
| `--desktop` | | For `code`: the desktop app, or stop if it cannot open |
| `--browser` | | For `code`: the browser, even when the desktop app is installed |
| `--server openvscode` | VS Code's own server | For `code` in the browser: openvscode-server, the open build, with extensions from Open VSX |
| `--no-browser` | | Print the address without opening it |

Ctrl-C ends the session and its job. With `--gpu`, so do 10 minutes with no browser tab or editor
connected, so the GPU goes back to others on `p1i`. On a GPU node, the session's server is not
open to the cluster network. The
first marimo, JupyterLab or VS Code session takes a minute to set up in `.aicentre` in your
project directory.

### Your own editor

`aicentre code` opens your desktop editor instead of the browser when it finds one: VS Code,
VSCodium, Cursor or Windsurf with a Remote-SSH extension, or Zed. With more than one, the one
named in `$VISUAL` or `$EDITOR` goes first. Any other editor works too:
name its command in your settings under `[desktop]`, with `{host}` and `{path}` (or `{urlpath}`,
for a URL) where the host and folder go. Whatever opens, aicentre prints the host and folder, so
any ssh tool can reach the same place. On the login node, the session lasts until you close the
editor.

On a GPU node, ask for the desktop app with `aicentre code --gpu --desktop`. Without `--desktop`
the browser opens. The desktop app there runs an ssh server of your own inside the job, and DTU
has not yet said that it allows one, so it is your choice to make. It also needs one line at the
top of `~/.ssh/config`, which `aicentre check` prints:

```text
Include ~/.local/state/aicentre/ssh_config
```

aicentre keeps that file up to date and never edits `~/.ssh/config` itself. With the aicentre
Home Manager module and `programs.ssh` enabled, the line is added for you; otherwise add the path
to `programs.ssh.includes`. Until the line is there, `aicentre code --gpu --desktop` stops
and says what is missing. The connection goes through the login node, and it is
encrypted all the way to the GPU node. Terminals in your editor see the job's GPU. Ctrl-C in the
terminal where you ran `aicentre code` ends the job. So do 10 minutes with the editor closed, so
the GPU goes back to others on `p1i`.

## Batch jobs

`aicentre` does not submit batch jobs. Open `aicentre shell` and use the scheduler: `bsub`,
`bjobs` and `bkill` on DTU, `sbatch`, `squeue` and `scancel` on Gefion. Those jobs show in
`aicentre list jobs` too. Versions before 0.2 had `aicentre submit`, `logs` and `cancel`; they now
say what to run instead.

## Moving data

```bash
aicentre push datasets              # datasets/ into your project directory
aicentre pull results               # results/ back to this folder
```

Both resume a stopped copy. The [moving data](/gallery/xfr-dat/) example covers
git, large transfers and NGC's staging area.

## Examples

```bash
aicentre gallery                    # the examples in the gallery
aicentre gallery get int-gpu        # one of them onto the cluster, in examples/int-gpu
```

`gallery get` downloads the example on the login node and prints what to run next.

## In your agent tool

`aicentre mcp` gives an agent tool you already use tools that read this site, your setup and
your jobs. Add it once:

```bash
claude mcp add --scope user aicentre -- aicentre mcp     # Claude Code
codex mcp add aicentre -- aicentre mcp                   # Codex
```

For opencode, add this to `opencode.json`:

```json
{
  "mcp": {
    "aicentre": { "type": "local", "command": ["aicentre", "mcp"] }
  }
}
```

Other agent tools that take an MCP server on stdio start it the same way: the command
`aicentre mcp`. Its tools only read: the docs, your jobs and your setup. The prompt under
[With your agent](#with-your-agent) installs `aicentre` and offers this step at the end.

Versions before 0.2 had `aicentre chat`, which kept model logins and conversations of its own.
`aicentre` no longer reads them. Remove them with
`rm -r ~/.config/aicentre/agent ~/.local/state/aicentre/chat`.

## On the clusters

`aicentre` keeps itself on each provider you use, at your laptop's version, in
`~/.local/bin/aicentre`. On a login node, the same commands use the scheduler directly:

```bash
aicentre list jobs
aicentre shell --gpu        # a GPU shell, from the login node
```

marimo and VS Code open in a browser, so start them from your laptop. Set `sync = false` under a provider in your settings
to keep `aicentre` off it.

## Upgrade and uninstall

```bash
aicentre upgrade
aicentre uninstall
```

`upgrade` switches to the latest release, and keeps your version if anything fails. It installs
only a release signed with aicentre's release key, and so does the install script. `uninstall`
asks before it removes your settings, and `--purge` skips the question. It ends with the command
that removes `aicentre` from each cluster.

## With Nix

Run it from this site's flake without installing:

```bash
nix run github:Pioneer-Centre-for-AI/p1-compute-documentation#aicentre -- check
```

To keep it, use the flake's Home Manager module. `settings` becomes
`~/.config/aicentre/config.toml`:

```nix
# flake.nix
inputs.p1-compute.url = "github:Pioneer-Centre-for-AI/p1-compute-documentation";

# your Home Manager configuration
imports = [ inputs.p1-compute.homeManagerModules.default ];
programs.aicentre = {
  enable = true;
  settings = {
    dtu.user = "s123456";
    gefion.user = "jdoe";
  };
};
```

On NixOS without Home Manager, use `nixosModules.default` with `programs.aicentre.enable = true`,
or `overlays.default` for `pkgs.aicentre`. Nix then handles updates, so `upgrade` and
`uninstall` leave it alone.

## Where it keeps files


| Path | What |
| --- | --- |
| `~/.local/bin/aicentre` | A link to the program |
| `~/.local/share/aicentre/` | The program itself |
| `~/.config/aicentre/` | Your settings |
| `~/.local/state/aicentre/` | State it keeps between runs, such as job notes |
| `~/.cache/aicentre/` | Downloads during an upgrade |
| `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/aicentre/` | Open logins to the providers, and other files that only live while it runs |

